## 10. Resource and Performance Requirements

The callback will execute within the Ansible execution process, so resource efficiency is essential.

Design requirements:

- No external HTTP calls during normal task execution.
- No persistent storage of complete Ansible result objects.
- No full stdout/stderr capture.
- Compact counters per host.
- Compact task timing aggregates.
- Bounded in-flight timing state.
- No unbounded lists of task events.
- No background worker threads.
- No separate telemetry daemon.
- No API call per task or per host during execution.
- One final publishing phase with bounded batches.
- Disable unnecessary detailed collection for unapproved jobs.

Support configurable telemetry profiles:

```yaml
profiles:
  standard:
    host_summary: true
    task_timings: false

  detailed:
    host_summary: true
    task_timings: true
    top_n: 20
```

Benchmark with the callback enabled and disabled at approximately 500 hosts, and then under representative larger workloads.

Measure:

- Peak memory usage.
- CPU overhead.
- Execution-time overhead.
- Callback event-processing overhead.
- Final publication duration.
- Behavior under concurrent AAP jobs.
- Behavior when Zabbix is unavailable.

A starting engineering target is less than 1% additional execution overhead during collection, excluding final publication. This is a target requiring measurement, not an established performance result.

